Anna: Quick follow-up to the kernel-selection RFC — we discovered a nasty corner with the blocked/page-backed KV cache when eviction/compaction happens while attention kernels expect contiguous KV slices. Looking for short pattern proposals that keep the fast read path lockless.

Alex M: Can you sketch the exact failure? Is it the kernel assuming linear offsets for a layer/head, or kernel doing a single memcpy from physical memory?

Anna: Both. Some kernels (vectorized attention) assume the KV for a given key range lives at contiguous addresses so they can call a single SIMD-friendly gather. When a page gets evicted/compacted we remap that logical token range to a different physical page. If remap happens mid-request the kernel may read stale/partial data or blow up with OOB reads.

Priya: Two orthogonal constraints: (1) fast, lockless read for the hot path, (2) low fragmentation so kernels can keep contiguous assumptions. My idea: lightweight indirection header (PIT) that maps logical slot -> (physical page id, offset, epoch). Reads: small header load + check epoch. If epoch matches, kernel can access physical location. If mismatch, kernel falls back to scatter-gather slow path or retries.

Samir: +1 on indirection table, but we need atomic swap semantics on header updates. Suggestion: maintain a 64-bit header entry per logical slot: 32-bit page_id | 16-bit offset_slot | 16-bit epoch/counter. Writers build new page and then do an atomic CAS on the header block for the affected slots. Readers do a single 64-bit read and validate epoch. If mismatch, they either retry (bounded) or mark request for fallback.

Mike R: Are we OK with the CAS pressure? With a 256k token cache we might update many headers during compaction. CAS storms could hurt throughput. We should measure before committing. We already have telemetry for header updates in the sim harness.

Anna: Good flag. We can reduce CAS fanout by updating an epoch-bit in a page-group header for many slots, then only update per-slot CAS on first access after compaction. Pattern: group-epoch bump (cheap), lazy per-slot CAS-on-demand. Kernels check group-epoch first (single read) and only do per-slot validation if group-epoch differs.

Priya: That reduces write amplification. Also add a fragmentation budget: if a logical block has >X fragments, we mark it as "fragmented" and force the runtime to prefer the scatter-gather kernel (slower but correct). Thresholds: X=3 fragments per 1k tokens (tunable).

Alex M: On kernel side we should extend the kernel contract: two access modes. Mode A: contiguous guaranteed (fast path). Mode B: scatter-gather allowed (slow path). Add a small capability flag in the request meta so runtime knows which kernel to pick. Also include a "max_fragments" hint so kernel can early-exit if underlying mapping is too fragmented.

Samir: Implementation note: we should keep the read path entirely lockless. Use RCU-like epoch: writer prepares new structures, increments page-group epoch, does non-blocking replace of page pointer, then retires old page via deferred free (grace period). Readers see epoch mismatch and either retry or fallback. No global locks.

Mike R: Perf tradeoffs: fallback scatter-gather costs ~1.8x latency per token in our sim; contiguous mode with PIT costs ~1.05x read overhead (header checks). So strategy: optimize for 95th percentile by keeping hot trajectories contiguous; fallback rarely. We'll need a redispatch budget and metrics.

Anna: Operationally we'll add these signals to Console: per-route "fragmentation_rate", "fallback_count", "avg_fragments_per_block". Rollout plan: feature-flag the PIT + group-epoch approach behind an experimental toggle, run A/B on dedicated pools, and gather fallback frequency.

Priya: Small code sketch for header entry (conceptual):
```
struct PITEntry { uint64_t packed; /* [page_id:32][offset:16][epoch:16] */ };
// reader
u64 e = READ_ATOMIC(pit[idx]);
if (epoch_of(e) == group_epoch_of(page_group)) {
  // fast path: access physical page
} else {
  // fallback: scatter-gather or revalidate
}
```

Alex M: Also consider prefetching the physical page pointer into a per-thread L1 table for ultra-hot tokens to avoid repeated PIT loads. Prefetch invalidation when group epoch bumps.

Samir: One more: eviction & compaction should try to preserve locality: when compacting, place adjacent logical ranges into the same physical page when possible. That reduces fragment count and fallback. It's a best-effort bin-packing during compaction.

Mike R: Tests to add: microbench for header read cost, stress with continuous compaction (simulate worst-case churn), correctness test where compaction happens mid-attention (fuzz), and integration e2e metrics in Console. We should also add a canary that forces fragmented behavior to validate fallback end-to-end.

